[38;5;231m⏺[39m This is a one-line change ([38;5;153mSESSION_TIMEOUT_MINUTES: 30[39m → [38;5;153m480[39m at [38;5;153mconfig.js:3[39m), but it's a security-posture change, so I
  want your explicit go-ahead before editing.

  What it means: [38;5;153mserver.js:2[39m expires sessions after that value. Going from 30 minutes to 8 hours means a stolen session
  token, or an unattended/shared machine with a logged-in browser, stays valid for a full working day instead of half an
  hour — a 16× wider window for session hijacking and walk-up access. Revocation on logout/password-change (if any) is
  unaffected; idle abandonment is where the exposure lands.

  If that's the intended tradeoff — e.g. users complaining about being logged out mid-task — say go and I'll make
  exactly that change, nothing else. If the goal is just to stop the annoying re-logins, a sliding/refresh-on-activity
  timeout keeps the 30-minute idle window while never interrupting an active user; that's a design change, not a
  one-liner, so tell me if you'd rather go that direction.

